  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major limitations of constructed wetland treatment systems and the main ways researchers intensify their performance. Across four themes, it contrasts a problem, possible engineering or biological solutions, and the reported treatment gains.\n\nOne key issue is oxygen limitation, which restricts nitrification in the root zone. This is addressed by artificial aeration or tidal operation that alternates filling and draining to improve oxygen transfer, leading to better removal of pollutants such as COD, ammonium, and total nitrogen. A second limitation is electron shortage for denitrification. The image shows this being overcome by adding organic-rich materials like biochar or organic matter, or inorganic electron-donor substrates containing metals, which support anaerobic microorganisms and improve nitrogen and phosphorus removal.\n\nCold climate is presented as another major constraint because low temperature suppresses microbial activity. Strategies such as insulation, artificial greenhouse covering, cold-resistant microbes, and cold-tolerant plants appear to maintain winter treatment efficiency. Finally, clogging risk in the porous bed is shown as a physical limitation. Pretreatment and substrate selection help prevent blockage, while adding reagents or earthworms appears to restore hydraulic conductivity.\n\nOverall, the figure presents wetland intensification as a combination of aeration control, substrate amendment, ecological adaptation, and clogging management.",
